    "content": "U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) is the largest federal law enforcement agency under the Department of Homeland Security, responsible for securing U.S. borders, regulating international trade, and facilitating travel. CBP officers and agents, numbering over 60,000, prevent illegal entry, drug smuggling, and agriculture pests at ports of entry. Key details about CBP: Core Mission: Secure U.S. borders against terrorists/weapons, enforce customs/immigration laws, and manage trade. Structure: Comprised of the Office of Field Operations (ports of entry), U.S. Border Patrol (between ports), and Air and Marine Operations. Role in Travel: Inspects over a million travelers daily and manages programs like Preclearance at 15 international locations. Contact/Info: Information is available via {Link: help.cbp.gov https://www.cbp.gov/travel/international-visitors/applying-admission-united-states} or by calling 877-CBP-5511. The agency was founded in 2003, bringing together agriculture specialists, customs officers, and border patrol agents.",
